#!/usr/bin/env node
"use strict";

// The `tokfold` command as npm installs it.
//
// This file is a launcher, not an implementation. It finds the prebuilt binary
// for this machine, runs it with the arguments it was given, and reproduces its
// result as faithfully as a parent process can. Everything tokfold actually does
// happens in the Rust binary.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why the child gets the real file descriptors
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `stdio: "inherit"` hands the child fds 0/1/2 directly instead of piping them
// through this process. That is load-bearing for three separate reasons:
//
//   * `mcp` streams. It is a line-framed protocol server that reads and
//     answers one message at a time, and piping it through Node would add a
//     buffering layer where an extra buffer can stall a session. (`compress`
//     and `expand` do not stream -- each reads its whole input and writes its
//     output in one call -- so for them this reason does not apply.)
//   * Broken pipes stay broken in the right place. `tokfold compress | head`
//     closes the pipe early, and the CLI treats that as a clean exit
//     (crates/tokfold-cli/src/main.rs). With a relay in the middle, the EPIPE
//     would land on Node instead, where the default is an unhandled error.
//   * Terminal detection keeps working. The child sees a tty when the user has
//     one, rather than always seeing a pipe.
//
// Only those three. On macOS Node starts the child with every other descriptor
// closed, so `--input /dev/fd/3` reaches the binary as a closed descriptor and
// fails with EBADF. Measured through a shell parent, `--input /dev/fd/3`
// with fd 3 open on a file: macOS, Node v22.23.2, both this launcher and the
// published 0.0.1 one (exit 2, "Bad file descriptor"; the binary run directly
// exits 0); Linux aarch64, glibc 2.39, Node v22.23.2 and v18.20.8, this
// launcher (fds 3 and 4 closed in the child). Listing more entries in `stdio` would not fix that safely: this
// process cannot tell a descriptor its caller passed from one Node opened for
// itself, and handing the binary Node's own would be worse than handing it none.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why the spawn is asynchronous
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `spawnSync` would be the shorter way to write this and it is the wrong one.
// It blocks the event loop for the whole life of the child, so no signal
// handler can run while the child is alive -- and with no handler installed,
// a signal sent to this process alone gets its default disposition and kills
// the launcher on the spot, leaving the child running, reparented to init, and
// still holding the caller's fds 0/1/2.
//
// That is not a corner case. It is what `timeout`, a cancelled CI job, systemd
// with `KillMode=process`, and any parent that calls `child.kill()` on a pid
// all do. For `tokfold mcp` it means an orphaned protocol server holding a
// client's pipes open after the client believes it killed the session.
//
// Ctrl-C never showed this, which is why it is worth spelling out: a terminal
// interrupt goes to the whole foreground process group, so the child was
// signalled directly and died on its own. Only a signal addressed to this pid
// alone produces the orphan.
//
// Spawning asynchronously keeps the event loop free, so the handlers below can
// forward the signal to the child and then wait for it to die. That holds for
// the signals in `FORWARDED`; see there for what happens with the others.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Exit codes
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// tokfold's exit codes are normative and documented: 0 success, 2 bad input,
// 3 a corrupt, empty, or otherwise unrecoverable archive. Scripts branch on
// them, so this launcher
// must never invent, remap or swallow one.
//
// When the launcher itself fails — unsupported platform, missing binary, a spawn
// that never got off the ground — it exits **1**, which is deliberately *not* one
// of tokfold's codes. It cannot be confused with "your input was bad" (2) or
// "that archive is corrupt" (3), and a script that already distinguishes 2 from 3
// does not silently treat an installation problem as a data problem.
//
// A child killed by a signal is reproduced by re-raising that signal on this
// process, so the calling shell sees a signal death rather than a number. The
// case where that cannot work — a signal the runtime ignores — falls back to
// the shell's own `128 + N` encoding, which keeps the signal identifiable and
// stays clear of 0, 1, 2 and 3. Node ignores SIGPIPE and SIGXFSZ; the binary can
// die of the second (a write past `ulimit -f`: 153 on macOS, as a shell reports
// for the binary run directly) but not of the first, because a Rust program
// starts with SIGPIPE ignored.
//
// All of this assumes the caller left each signal at its default disposition.
// A signal the caller *ignores* is not ignored through this launcher: the
// listeners below replace the inherited ignore, removing them restores the
// default rather than the ignore, and Node starts the child with every signal at
// its default. So `nohup tokfold mcp &` dies on a hangup that the binary run
// directly survives, and so does a background job whose shell started it with
// SIGINT ignored (measured on macOS, Node 22; the 0.0.1 launcher does the
// same). Node offers no way to read an inherited disposition, so this is
// documented rather than fixed.
//
// So `1` means "tokfold never ran" in every case production was measured to
// reach. The signal path has a `1` of its own, but it needs Node to hand this
// process a signal *name* that its own `os.constants.signals` table cannot map
// back to a number. The name is produced in Node's C++ layer, not read from
// that table, so the two could in principle disagree. Measured, they do not:
// on macOS with Node 22 for any of signals 1-31, and on Linux aarch64 (glibc
// 2.39, Node v22.23.2) for any of signals 1-64 -- every name Node reported
// maps back, and the signals it cannot name arrive unnamed, below. Only a test
// that empties the table reaches this `1`. It is
// still handled, and it is the one launcher exit that writes a line saying so,
// which is what would let a caller tell it apart from a launcher that really
// never started anything.
//
// That table is not the only place a name can go missing, and the other place
// is worse. Node turns the child's signal number into a name before the `exit`
// event fires, and a signal it has no name for -- SIGEMT on macOS; on Linux
// aarch64 (glibc 2.39, Node v22.23.2) every signal from 32 to 64, which is the
// 31 realtime signals from SIGRTMIN = 34 plus the two glibc reserves below
// them; all measured -- does not arrive as a signal at all: it arrives as
// `exit(0, null)`, exactly what a successful run looks like. The launcher then exits **0** for a binary
// that was killed. No public API tells the two apart, so this is documented
// rather than fixed; the published 0.0.1 launcher does the same. Nothing in
// tokfold raises such a signal, so it takes an outside `kill` to reach it.
//
// The alternative — `128 + undefined`, i.e. `NaN` — is worse than ambiguous,
// and how much worse depends on the runtime. On Node 18, the `engines` floor,
// `process.exit(NaN)` is accepted silently and reports **0**: a killed run
// becomes a success. Node 20 added range validation and throws
// `ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE` there instead. So the guard below reads as superstition on
// a modern Node and is load-bearing on the oldest one this package supports.
// The Node 18 CI leg runs on Linux only (the matrix is linux-node18,
// linux-node22, macos-node22, windows-node22), so no leg runs this file on
// Node 18 under macOS -- see "Known issue" in this header.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Known issue, not fixed: an intermittent hang on Node 18 under macOS
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// One measurement (macOS arm64, Node v18.20.8, the fake binary in `hold`
// mode, a signal from FORWARDED, 60 runs per signal, a harness that kills the
// launcher after 8 s) found 32 of 300 runs in which the binary was already
// dead and reaped and the `exit` event below never fired, so the launcher sat
// until the harness killed it, with nothing on stderr. The same session
// reproduced it without tokfold, with a minimal script spawning /bin/sleep
// (5 of 60; 12 of 60 with these listeners installed), which points at Node 18's
// child_process on macOS rather than at this file. It saw no hang on macOS
// with Node v20.20.2, v22.23.2 or v26.7.0, nor on Linux with v18.20.8 or
// v22.23.2. A second attempt on the same host and Node version, 60 runs per
// signal, reproduced none; under CPU load (four `yes` processes) the npm
// suite on Node v18.20.8 then lost 1 to 3 of its 76 tests to the 30 s bound
// in 2 of 3 runs, and v20.20.2 and v22.23.2 passed all 76 under the same
// load. The published 0.0.1 launcher uses the same
// asynchronous `spawn` (its FORWARDED lacks only SIGUSR1), and whether it hangs
// the same way was not measured. CI cannot see it (no macos-node18 leg). The
// remedy -- a CI leg, a higher `engines` floor, or a watchdog here -- is an
// owner decision and none has been taken.

const fs = require("node:fs");
const os = require("node:os");
const { spawn } = require("node:child_process");
const { resolveBinaryPath } = require("../lib/resolve.js");

/** Exit code for a launcher-level failure. Never one of tokfold's own codes. */
const LAUNCHER_FAILED = 1;

/**
 * Signals worth relaying to the child before this process goes away.
 *
 * The first four are the ones a supervisor actually sends to ask for a clean
 * shutdown. SIGUSR1 is relayed for a different reason: with no listener, Node
 * does not die of it but opens its inspector on 127.0.0.1:9229 and prints
 * "Debugger listening" on stderr, so a SIGUSR1 sent to the launcher alone
 * started a debugger in it while the binary kept running. A listener suppresses
 * that (measured on Node 20 and 22), and relaying it gives the binary the death
 * it would have died run directly, which the exit handler then re-raises.
 *
 * A listener cannot cover the launcher's whole life. Node installs its own
 * SIGUSR1 handler while it boots, before this file runs, so a SIGUSR1 that lands
 * in that stretch still opens the inspector; one that lands earlier still, before
 * Node's handler exists, kills the launcher by the kernel default. Neither can
 * orphan anything -- there is no binary yet, though an inspector opened then
 * stays open while the launcher goes on to start one -- and the listeners below are
 * installed before the binary is looked up, which leaves only Node's boot and
 * this file's own `require`s in the stretch. `--disable-sigusr1` would close
 * it, and the launcher does not pass it: Node v18.20.8 and v20.20.2 refuse the
 * flag with exit 9, on the command line and in `NODE_OPTIONS` alike, while
 * v22.23.x and v26.7.0 accept it in both places (measured on macOS), so it
 * cannot go on the `#!` line of a package whose floor is Node 18. A caller on
 * Node 22 or later can set it through `NODE_OPTIONS` without touching that line.
 *
 * Nothing else is relayed, and what the launcher does with anything else is
 * Node's default, not the kernel's. SIGUSR2 and SIGALRM were measured to kill
 * it and leave the binary running; SIGPIPE and SIGXFSZ are ignored by Node, so
 * the launcher lives on and so does the binary.
 * SIGKILL is absent because it cannot be caught, so a `kill -9` on the launcher
 * still orphans the child -- that is a property of SIGKILL and no launcher can
 * fix it.
 *
 * Windows supports a different subset, and it does not refuse the same names at
 * both ends. Listening is permissive -- `SIGHUP` is accepted there and mapped
 * onto `CTRL_CLOSE_EVENT` -- while delivery is not: libuv's `kill` handles only
 * `SIGTERM`, `SIGKILL`, `SIGINT` and `0`, and anything else comes back as
 * `ENOSYS`, which Node raises as a throw. So both ends need guarding, and the
 * one that matters is the second: a throw out of a signal handler is an
 * uncaught exception that kills the launcher and leaves the binary running.
 */
const FORWARDED = ["SIGINT", "SIGTERM", "SIGHUP", "SIGQUIT", "SIGUSR1"];

/**
 * How many EAGAINs in a row `writeStderr` will sit through before giving up,
 * and how long it pauses between them. A chosen budget, not a measured
 * duration: no test reaches the retry (it needs a full non-blocking pipe on
 * fd 2). At the nominal pause the two multiply to about a second; how long
 * the retry really lasts was never measured.
 */
const EAGAIN_ATTEMPTS = 1000;
const EAGAIN_PAUSE_MS = 1;

/**
 * Blocks this thread for `ms`, without turning the event loop.
 *
 * The write below is synchronous on purpose, so anything it waits on has to be
 * synchronous too: `setTimeout` needs an event-loop turn, and every caller here
 * is on its way to `process.exit`, which never gives it one. `Atomics.wait` on
 * a location nobody else touches is the supported way to sleep a thread in
 * Node, and unlike in a browser it is permitted on the main thread.
 *
 * @param {number} ms
 */
function pause(ms) {
  Atomics.wait(new Int32Array(new SharedArrayBuffer(4)), 0, 0, ms);
}

/**
 * Writes to stderr and is done writing by the time it returns.
 *
 * `process.stderr.write` is documented as *asynchronous* when stderr is a TTY
 * on Windows, and `process.exit` does not wait for a pending asynchronous
 * write. Every message this launcher produces is followed immediately by an
 * exit, so on a Windows console the one explanation the user gets could arrive
 * truncated or not at all, leaving nothing but a bare exit code 1 to act on.
 * `fs.writeSync` has no such gap on any platform.
 *
 * The loop is not defensive padding: a short write is legal, and EAGAIN is
 * reachable whenever fd 2 is a non-blocking pipe, which is the caller's choice
 * and not ours. EPIPE means nobody is reading, which is not a failure worth
 * reporting -- there is nowhere left to report it to.
 *
 * The EAGAIN retry is both paused and bounded. A reader that never drains is a
 * real state -- a non-blocking pipe whose far end is alive but stopped -- and
 * an unpaused, unbounded retry answers it by pinning a core
 * for as long as it lasts, turning a diagnostic message into a hang. The
 * message is worth a bounded wait -- the budget is `EAGAIN_ATTEMPTS` pauses
 * of `EAGAIN_PAUSE_MS`, chosen and not measured; it is not worth the command
 * never returning. Progress resets the count, so a reader draining slowly is
 * waited out however long it takes. None of this reaches a *blocking* fd 2 --
 * the ordinary pipe, or a terminal stopped with Ctrl-S once its buffer fills --
 * because there
 * `fs.writeSync` never sees EAGAIN: it waits in the kernel, and nothing here
 * bounds that wait.
 *
 * @param {string} message
 */
function writeStderr(message) {
  const buffer = Buffer.from(message, "utf8");
  let written = 0;
  let stalls = 0;

  while (written < buffer.length) {
    try {
      written += fs.writeSync(2, buffer, written);
      stalls = 0;
    } catch (error) {
      if (error.code === "EAGAIN") {
        if (stalls >= EAGAIN_ATTEMPTS) {
          return;
        }
        stalls += 1;
        pause(EAGAIN_PAUSE_MS);
        continue;
      }
      // Anything else: stderr is unusable, so there is no channel left to
      // explain that stderr is unusable. Give up rather than turn a failed
      // message into a second, noisier failure.
      return;
    }
  }
}

/** The child, once there is one. Null until the spawn call returns. */
let child = null;

/**
 * Relays one signal to the child.
 *
 * `child` is never null here, although the handlers are installed before the
 * binary is even looked up. Node runs a signal listener on the event loop, never
 * inside a synchronous call, and everything from here to the end of `spawn` is
 * synchronous: a signal the kernel delivers meanwhile is queued and dispatched
 * after `spawn` returns, by which point `child` is set -- or the lookup or the
 * spawn has already failed and exited `1`, and the queued signal never runs.
 * An earlier version kept a `pending` slot for that signal and replayed it after
 * the spawn; the slot could never be filled, and it is gone.
 *
 * @param {NodeJS.Signals} signal
 */
function forward(signal) {
  try {
    // Best effort by design. The child may have exited already, in which case
    // this is a no-op and its `exit` event is what ends us.
    child.kill(signal);
  } catch {
    try {
      // The name is not deliverable on this platform -- Windows, where only
      // SIGTERM/SIGKILL/SIGINT survive the trip. Ask for termination in the
      // way this platform can express instead, because the promise being kept
      // is that the binary does not outlive the launcher, not that a
      // particular signal number reaches it.
      child.kill();
    } catch {
      // Already gone, or not signallable at all. Either way the `exit` handler
      // below is what ends this process, and a throw escaping a signal handler
      // here would be the very orphan this file exists to prevent.
    }
  }
}

// Installed *before* the spawn, not after, and before the binary is looked up.
// Signals are delivered by the kernel whenever they like, and between forking a
// child and registering a handler the default disposition still applies -- a
// SIGTERM landing in that window would kill the launcher outright and leave the
// binary running on the caller's descriptors. Handlers first closes the window,
// and a signal that lands during the lookup or the spawn is dispatched once
// `spawn` returns -- see `forward`. Ahead of the lookup, because that shortens
// the stretch in which a SIGUSR1 opens Node's inspector -- see `FORWARDED`.
const installed = [];
for (const signal of FORWARDED) {
  try {
    process.on(signal, () => forward(signal));
    installed.push(signal);
  } catch {
    // This platform does not support listening for that signal. Nothing to
    // relay, and nothing that needs saying about it.
  }
}

let binary;
try {
  binary = resolveBinaryPath();
} catch (error) {
  writeStderr(`${error.message}\n`);
  process.exit(LAUNCHER_FAILED);
}

/**
 * The binary could not be started at all: not on disk, not executable, not a
 * program this kernel can run, or the exec failed for some other reason.
 *
 * One function because the same failure reaches this process by two different
 * routes -- see the spawn below -- and a user who sees one of them should not
 * be able to tell which route it took.
 *
 * @param {Error} error
 * @returns {never}
 */
function failedToRun(error) {
  writeStderr(
    `tokfold: failed to run the binary at ${binary}\n${error.message}\n`,
  );
  process.exit(LAUNCHER_FAILED);
}

try {
  child = spawn(binary, process.argv.slice(2), { stdio: "inherit" });
} catch (error) {
  // `spawn` reports most start-up failures asynchronously, on the `error` event
  // below -- but not all of them, and the exceptions are not obscure. Node
  // defers exactly EACCES, EAGAIN, EMFILE, ENFILE and ENOENT; every other errno
  // from `uv_spawn` is thrown from this call. That includes **ENOEXEC**: the
  // file is present, is executable, and is not something this kernel can
  // execute -- a truncated download, a partially written package, a binary for
  // the wrong architecture. Without this catch the user gets a Node stack trace
  // naming `child_process.js`, and exit code 1 by accident rather than by
  // contract.
  failedToRun(error);
}

child.on("error", (error) => {
  // Once the child has a pid it was started, and the only `error` left for
  // this file to see is a relayed signal that could not be delivered:
  // `child.kill` reports EPERM on this event instead of throwing it. Treating
  // that as a failure to start exited `1` for a binary that was running, and
  // left it running with the caller's descriptors -- the orphan `forward`
  // exists to prevent. Its `exit` event still ends this process.
  if (child.pid !== undefined) {
    return;
  }
  failedToRun(error);
});

child.on("exit", (code, signal) => {
  // The child was killed by a signal rather than exiting. Re-raising the same
  // signal on ourselves is the only way the *parent* shell sees what really
  // happened: a shell reports `130` for SIGINT because the process died by that
  // signal, not because it exited with 130. Translating the signal into some
  // number here would lose that distinction, and `Ctrl-C` on a long compression
  // would look like an ordinary failure.
  if (signal) {
    // The handlers above replaced the default disposition for the signals they
    // cover, and a signal with a listener attached does not kill anything.
    // Dropping them restores the default, which is what makes the re-raise
    // below fatal instead of a no-op that loops straight back into `kill`.
    //
    // That holds for SIGUSR1 too, though it looks as if it should not. Node's
    // inspector handler is not something a listener stacks on top of: adding
    // the listener replaced it, and removing the listener leaves the kernel
    // default behind, not the inspector. So the re-raise kills this process by
    // SIGUSR1 like any other signal (measured on macOS with Node 20, 22 and 26),
    // and a SIGUSR1 arriving from outside after this point ends it the same way.
    // The `0.0.1` launcher re-raised SIGUSR1 while Node's handler was still
    // installed, which is what opened the inspector.
    for (const name of installed) {
      process.removeAllListeners(name);
    }

    process.kill(process.pid, signal);

    // Reached for a signal that is ignored or cannot be delivered -- Node
    // ignores SIGPIPE and SIGXFSZ process-wide, for instance. Exiting `1` here would
    // say "the launcher failed", which is the one thing that did not happen:
    // the binary ran and was killed. `128 + N` is the shell's own encoding of
    // "died by signal N", so a caller that cannot observe the signal itself
    // still learns which signal it was, and still gets a code outside
    // tokfold's own 0/2/3.
    //
    // The number comes from Node's table rather than a literal, and the lookup
    // is guarded -- but not for the reason it is tempting to give. The name
    // was produced by Node's C++ layer, which keeps its own list rather than
    // reading this table; every name that list produced maps back here
    // (measured on macOS with Node 22 for signals 1-31, and on Linux aarch64,
    // glibc 2.39, Node v22.23.2, for signals 1-64), and Windows carrying a much
    // smaller table than POSIX does not by itself change that. The
    // guard is there because the cost of a miss is out of all proportion to how
    // likely it is, and because the miss is silent: an unmapped name produces
    // `128 + undefined`, which is `NaN`. `process.exit(NaN)`
    // is accepted on Node 18 -- this package's `engines` floor -- and reports
    // **0**, turning a killed run into a success; Node 20 and later throw
    // `ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE` instead. Both outcomes are wrong, and neither is what
    // the caller should see, so the miss is handled explicitly.
    const number = os.constants.signals[signal];
    if (!Number.isInteger(number)) {
      // The one launcher-level exit that did not fail to launch anything. Say
      // so, or `1` is indistinguishable from "the binary was never found" and
      // the operator debugs the wrong half of the system.
      writeStderr(
        `tokfold: the binary was killed by ${signal}, which this runtime cannot map to a signal number\n`,
      );
      process.exit(LAUNCHER_FAILED);
    }
    process.exit(128 + number);
  }

  // Normal termination: hand back the child's own code, untouched. `code` is
  // null only in the signal case already handled above; the fallback keeps a
  // null from becoming a silent 0, which would report success for a run that
  // did not finish.
  process.exit(code === null ? LAUNCHER_FAILED : code);
});
